Challenge Overview

    Name: DISKO 4

    Description: "Can you find the flag in this disk image? This time I deleted the file!"

    File: disko-4.dd (FAT32 disk image)

    Goal: Recover the deleted file containing the flag

📥 Step 1: Initial Setup

First, let's download and prepare the disk image:
bash

# Navigate to Downloads directory
cd ~/Downloads

# Extract the compressed disk image
gunzip disko-4.dd.gz

# Verify the file type
file disko-4.dd

Output: disko-4.dd: DOS/MBR boot sector, ... FAT (32 bit)
🛠️ Step 2: Method 1 - Using fatcat (Quickest Solution)

fatcat is a specialized forensic tool for FAT filesystems that can easily list and recover deleted files.
Install fatcat (if needed)
bash

sudo apt update && sudo apt install fatcat -y

List all files including deleted ones
bash

fatcat disko-4.dd -l / -d

Output shows a directory called log/
Check contents of the log directory
bash

fatcat disko-4.dd -l /log -d

Output reveals a deleted file: ?lag.txt (the ? indicates it's deleted)
Recover the deleted flag file
bash

fatcat disko-4.dd -r /log/flag.txt > flag.txt

View the recovered flag
bash

cat flag.txt

Flag: picoCTF{...} (the actual flag will be displayed)
🔧 Step 3: Method 2 - Using testdisk (Interactive Approach)

If fatcat isn't available, testdisk provides a user-friendly interface for recovery.
Install testdisk (if needed)
bash

sudo apt update && sudo apt install testdisk -y

Run testdisk on the disk image
bash

testdisk disko-4.dd

Navigate through testdisk:

    Select the disk → [Proceed]

    Choose partition table type → [None] (since it's a raw FAT32 filesystem)

    Select filesystem type → [FAT32]

    Choose menu option → [Advanced] → Filesystem Utils

    Select the FAT32 partition → [Undelete]

    Browse directories:

        You'll see a log/ directory

        Press → to enter the log directory

    Find the deleted file:

        Inside log, you'll see ?lag.txt (the ? shows it's deleted)

        Navigate to it using arrow keys

        Press c to copy/recover the file

        Choose destination (current directory)

        Press C to confirm

    Exit testdisk → Press q multiple times

View the recovered flag
bash

cat flag.txt
